#Terraform Architecture & Workflow:
•Terraform uses HCL to code.
•Terraform scripts must be saved with a .tf extension.

#Core Execution Lifecycle;
The standard sequence of commands runs as follows:
Save script to .tf → terraform init → terraform fmt → terraform validate → terraform plan → terraform apply

[ ec2.tf ] ──> [ terraform init ] ──> [ terraform plan ] ──> [ terraform apply ]
                      │
                      ├──> ( terraform fmt )
                      │
                      └──> ( terraform validate )

               /\
              /  \
             /    \
            <      >  [ terraform destroy ]
             \    /
              \  /
               \/
#Note: terraform fmt and terraform validate are optional but highly recommended steps.

#Detailed Command Breakdown
•We have a Terraform script saved with a .tf extension. The lifecycle commands operate as follows:
•terraform init: To initialize the Terraform script and the working directory.
•terraform fmt: Formats the Terraform script's indentation and spacing (it will get the script in proper order).
•terraform validate: Verifies and validates the Terraform script syntax.
•terraform plan: Creates an execution plan for the script, previewing changes before they hit the cloud.
•terraform apply: Creates actual resources on the cloud based on the given plan.
•terraform destroy: It will destroy / delete the resources which got created in the cloud.

#Best Practices & Workspace Setup:
It is highly recommended to create a dedicated directory/folder for your scripts to keep things isolated:

sudo mkdir 01-tf-script
cd 01-tf-script

